~ the escape hatch for everything the declarative model can't express:
~ plain JavaScript between <% and %>, run through the `lh` bridge object.
~ a <% ... %> block executes against your data; @scriptCall(fn) drops a
~ function's return value into prose; an inline <% expr %> splices a value.

#findings: [
  { claim - Attention precedes perception, supported - true, strength - strong }
  { claim - Looking is never neutral, supported - true, strength - strong }
  { claim - Attention is a form of labour, supported - true, strength - moderate }
  { claim - Perception is neutral, supported - false, strength - contested }
]

<%
// lh.data — every #thing: definition, as plain JS objects
const findings = lh.data.findings;
const supported = findings.filter(f => f.supported === true).length;
const pct = Math.round((supported / findings.length) * 100);

// lh.prose() — the prose text nodes, with helpers like .wordCount()
const words = lh.prose().wordCount();

// expose readouts as named functions, ready for @scriptCall below
function claimStats() { return supported + ' of ' + findings.length + ' findings supported (' + pct + '%)'; }
function readingTime() { return '~' + Math.ceil(words / 200) + ' min to read'; }

// other bridge calls available, not exercised here:
//   lh.set(path, value)  update a value in the data
//   lh.sort(name, fn)    reorder every instance of a node type
//   lh.query(name)       every node of a type, as { params, content }
//   lh.node(id)          a single node by its id parameter
%>

For three years I kept my references in a database. Every paper I read,
I logged — author, title, year, the lot.

~ @scriptCall(fn) evaluates a function defined above and inlines the result

Supported findings: @scriptCall(claimStats). Reading time: @scriptCall(readingTime).

~ an inline <% ... %> expression is spliced straight into the prose

There are <% lh.data.findings.length %> findings in all, and by my own
count the lamp has been lit <% 11 * 24 %> hours.
